**Clarify first:** does `*` match exactly one segment (assumed here)? What if both `/bar/*` and `/bar/a` match: is exact preferred? What should `callRoute` return for no match (null, `Optional`, or an exception)? Is the path case-sensitive? How are trailing slashes handled? Can a route be overwritten?

**Step 1: hashmap version.** `Map<String, String>` from the path to the result. O(L) per call for hashing a path of length L. It works until wildcards arrive.

**Step 2: trie keyed by segments.** Split the path on `/` (ignoring empty segments) and store one trie node per segment; the node at the end of a route holds the result. A segment `*` is stored as an ordinary child key `"*"`.

```java
class Router {
    private static class Node {
        Map<String, Node> next = new HashMap<>();
        String result;
    }
    private final Node root = new Node();

    void addRoute(String path, String result) {
        Node cur = root;
        for (String seg : split(path)) cur = cur.next.computeIfAbsent(seg, k -> new Node());
        cur.result = result;
    }
    String callRoute(String path) { return match(root, split(path), 0); }

    private String match(Node n, String[] segs, int i) {
        if (i == segs.length) return n.result;
        Node exact = n.next.get(segs[i]);
        if (exact != null) {
            String r = match(exact, segs, i + 1);
            if (r != null) return r;           // backtrack if the exact branch fails
        }
        Node wild = n.next.get("*");
        return wild == null ? null : match(wild, segs, i + 1);
    }
}
```
The backtracking matters: with routes `/a/b/c` and `/a/*/d`, the call `/a/b/d` must fall back from the exact `b` branch to `*`.

**Complexity:** each node has at most two matching children (exact and `*`), so a call is O(S) for S segments in the common case and at most O(2^S) in the worst case, which is acceptable for short paths. Space is O(total segments).

**Make it extensible:** make the result type generic (`Router<T>`), put parsing in a `PathParser`, and allow `removeRoute`. If extra features come (path parameters like `/users/:id`, a `**` wildcard, HTTP methods), the same trie extends by adding node types and match rules. For concurrent use, use a `ReadWriteLock` or an immutable snapshot.

**JUnit tests:** exact route, root path, unknown route, wildcard in the middle, exact over wildcard, backtracking case, trailing slash, overwrite, empty path.
